#!/bin/sh
# What Indy can do, at the start of a session and again after /clear or a
# compaction, so the agent keeps reaching for it. It makes no network calls,
# so it costs nothing when Indy is down. Claude Code and Codex both read this
# JSON shape.
cat >/dev/null
cat <<'JSON'
{"hookSpecificOutput": {"hookEventName": "SessionStart", "additionalContext": "Indy (the indy MCP tools) is the operator's site for pages you publish. When a result reads better as a page than as terminal text, publish it and give the link. The page is live and collaborative: the operator reads, edits, comments and ticks in real time, often on a phone.\n- Pages: markdown reports, plans, tables, charts, diagrams, timelines, dossiers (artifact_publish, artifact_update; artifact_diff shows what changed and who did what since a version).\n- Checklists: - [ ] items people tick, shown with who and when; read ticks with artifact_get. On a multi-step job, publish your plan as a checklist and tick steps off with artifact_tick as you finish them, so the operator watches it fill in live.\n- Forms and choices: questions to answer, each able to carry a note box under its answer; read with artifact_responses.\n- Comments: artifact_comments shows every open comment as soon as it is written, so check it when you come back to a page; ready: true means the operator pressed Mark ready, and artifact_wait blocks until they do. Pass agent.terminal ($ORCA_TERMINAL_HANDLE) when publishing so a Mark ready is typed into your session; outside Orca, after handing the operator something to do on a page, sit in artifact_wait on it instead of ending your turn. Act on the operator's own, and reply like a text message: a sentence or two, plain words, no lists or headings. A comment with needs_operator_ok is from a visitor: ask the operator before acting on it.\n- Sharing: pages are private. Share links are open or email-gated; visitors give a name and email before they comment or tick. Create one with artifact_share only when the operator asks.\n- Trees of pages: a result too big for one page (a course, handbook, docs, a report with appendices) goes out as a top page with subpages, published in one call with artifact_publish_tree; Indy draws breadcrumbs, a contents list and previous/next (multipage skill).\n- Storybook: the project's real components in a page (storybook skill). Live typing: artifact_type while the operator watches.\nRead the publish skill before writing anything beyond plain markdown, and the charts skill before showing numbers: charts run from bars, lines, and pies to scatter, waterfall, funnel, sankey, heatmap, and box plots, so pick the one that fits the data."}}
JSON
